# Server
PORT=3001
NODE_ENV=development
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000

# Same production Postgres as the root Next.js app (plan §1) — pooled
# (PgBouncer) connection string, matching lib/db/prisma.ts's convention.
# Session 3-1 finding: pgbouncer (the Railway service) has NO native
# DATABASE_URL variable to reference — it's a bespoke PgBouncer deploy
# (Session 1-3b), not a Railway-managed template. The Postgres service
# itself exposes a scoped credential for this purpose,
# CORE_APP_DB_PASSWORD (paired with MONEY_SVC_DB_PASSWORD for the future
# money-service — Session 1-3b's per-service credential pattern), meant
# to be combined with pgbouncer's private domain
# (pgbouncer.railway.internal) into a real connection string. Davin sets
# this directly on the Railway service (never through an agent, never
# printed) — see docs/secret-matrix.md.
DATABASE_URL=

# Redis for the ThrottlerGuard's storage (own instance — see railway.toml).
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379

# Shared with the Next.js app (docs/secret-matrix.md) — never as a CLI arg,
# never printed. Granted via Railway's variable-set mechanism once this
# service exists (Session 3-1, Step 6).
NEXTAUTH_SECRET=
